---
description: Conventional Commit, pull-request, release-note, and PR-review conventions for this repository
alwaysApply: true
---

When writing a git commit message, a pull request, or a release note, or reviewing someone
else's pull request, follow `AGENTS.md` at the repository root.

- Read `git diff --staged` — the staged diff is the source of truth, not filenames.
- Choose an accurate Conventional Commit type and scope; write an imperative subject
  (≤ 50 chars ideally, 72 hard; no trailing period).
- Add a body only when it explains *why* the change was made, not *how*.
- Notate breaking changes with `!` and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.
- Add `BREAKING CHANGE:` and issue references (`Closes #123`) when relevant and true; never
  guess an issue number.
- If nothing is staged, say so and stop — never `git add -A`. If asked to choose what to
  stage, select only the files that form one coherent commit, not everything at once.
- For a pull request (only when asked), detect the base branch (main/master), read the
  branch's diff against it, and write a title plus a description with summary, what
  changed, testing, and breaking changes, as plain Markdown.
- For a release note (only when asked), fetch tags first, base the range on the last
  version tag reachable from `HEAD`, and report any disagreement with the project's
  version field instead of silently picking one. Read the diff, not just the subjects;
  group entries by user-visible effect; match the style of prior releases over any
  template; drop empty sections rather than writing "None". If the repository has no
  versioning at all, say so and stop — never create its first tag unprompted.
- For a review of someone else's pull request (only when asked), read it through `gh` —
  diff, checks, existing threads, commits and their authors, merge method — and separate
  what actually blocks the merge (breaks the build or a test, loses data, opens a security
  hole, breaks a documented contract, or doesn't do what the PR claims) from what is only
  a suggestion. Calibrate the bar to the project; never manufacture a blocker; when nothing
  blocks, say so in one plain line first. On a fork PR, empty `gh pr checks` output means
  the workflows have not run, not that they passed. Write one Markdown block for the
  decision made — the review comment, or the merge-commit message.
- Never mutate git on your own: propose the exact `git add` / `git commit` / PR commands
  and run them only when explicitly asked; commit the staged set as-is. Tagging and
  publishing a release take the autonomous mode *and* an explicit request to publish —
  never overwrite or move an existing tag or release.
- Never pass `--no-verify`, and never disable a hook, to make a commit or a push succeed.
  A hook that fails is a finding, and it stops the work.
- Work in the repository that you did not create is another writer's state: inspect it,
  never sweep it into a commit, never rewrite it. A rejected push means another writer
  moved the branch — fetch, read the divergence, and report the exact commits on each
  side; never rebase or merge it on your own.
- Every stop hands the work back with five fields: the goal and the exact blocked step;
  what you attempted, and the git output, verbatim; the causes you eliminated, and how;
  the one decision needed from the user; the state that remains, and whether it is safe
  to leave. In the autonomous mode, claim completion only from `git log -1 --format=full`
  and `git status`, run after the push — a claim without that output is not a completion.
- The autonomous mode never extends to a review: "approve it" or "yes, merge" tells you
  what the block should say, not to run it — never run `gh pr review`, `gh pr comment`,
  `gh pr merge`, or `gh pr close`.
- For repository work (only when asked) — branches, merges and rebases, history editing,
  undo, moving work, conflicts, recovery — read the real state, say what the situation is,
  and present the exact commands. Never rewrite published history on a shared branch; never
  force-push, and use `--force-with-lease` only when asked to update your own pushed branch
  after a rebase; never discard uncommitted work (`reset --hard`, `checkout --`, `restore`
  without `--staged`, `clean`, `stash drop`) without explicit confirmation; never resolve a
  conflict by picking a side to end it; never leave a rebase, merge, cherry-pick, or bisect
  part-way through without naming the state and the command that ends it. State the reflog
  entry that recovers the work before any command that can lose it.
- A trailer has exactly two sources: the user's words this session, and the squash-merge
  transcription. Never take one from your own identity, the model or tool name,
  `commit.template`, a `prepare-commit-msg` or `commit-msg` hook, `GIT_AUTHOR_*` or
  `GIT_COMMITTER_*`, a CI variable, an editor plugin, or prior commits. Never pass
  `--author`, `-c user.name`, or `-c user.email`, and never write to git config. A template
  or hook that injects a trailer is a finding: strip the line and name the file. After every
  commit you make, read `git log -1 --format=full` and amend out any unrequested attribution
  line at once; run the same check across `@{u}..HEAD` before a push and stop on a hit.
- No attribution trailers by default, in every mode: no `Co-authored-by:`, `Signed-off-by:`,
  `Reviewed-by:`, or AI or agent identity. Add one only when the user asks in the session or
  a standing instruction exists in this repository's agent context file — never inferred from
  history, branch names, or the diff, and never with an invented name or email. One exception:
  in a squash-merge message, transcribe `Co-authored-by:` lines from the branch's real commit
  metadata, since the squash would otherwise destroy authorship that already exists.

See `AGENTS.md` for the complete workflow, type taxonomy, and examples.
